            <div type="bibliography" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en">
                <p>
                <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneDVC"/> <citedRange>8B</citedRange>.</bibl>
                    </p>
                      <p>Cf. 
                      <bibl><ptr target="#AnagnostopoulouGalaniMenti2022"/>, <citedRange>243 n. 15</citedRange></bibl> (on oracular questions about professional activity).
                    </p>
                          </div>
            <div type="edition" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc">
                    <ab>
                        <lb n="1"/>προβατεύ  μ; 
                        </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en">
                <p>Shall I work as a shepherd or not?</p>
            </div>
                <div type="translation" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="es">
              <p>¿Me dedico al pastoreo o no?</p>
                    </div>
                <div type="commentary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en"> 
                    <p>For questions concerning animal husbandry (a frequent topic of enquiry in the Dodona oracular corpus), see the commentaries of the first editors and <bibl><ptr target="#AnagnostopoulouGalaniMenti2022"/>, <citedRange>243</citedRange>. </bibl></p>
                    
   <p n="Ithaca Results">Ithaca identifies Epirus as the most probable geographical origin of the inscription (40.77%), followed by the Saronic Gulf, Corinthia, and the Argolid (33.12%). Chronologically, the highest probability is assigned to the interval 550-540 BC, although the interval 450-440 BC receives a nearly identical probability.
   See the <ref target="https://github.com/IDodone/IDodoneOnline/tree/main/ithaca-results/IDodoneOnline8B">Ithaca results and associated files</ref>.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
